To solve the confirmation questions, I propose changing the commands in
nethack a bit.  This will probably offend some rogue/nethack purists, but
please bear with me and consider the benefits.

I propose that ordinary move commands not attack or enter water (the command
is ignored in these cases).  To attack a monster, a command enters attack
mode ('f' for fight, perhaps...whatever).  In attack mode, the move commands
hit, and a hit to a blank square does cost a turn (what if an invisible
monster is there, and you are swinging at it).  Some other key could
switch you back to move mode (maybe 'm'), or else the same key could
be used as a toggle, ala '@'.  To swim, you use the #swim command, which
should be duly documented.

WHY??  I hear you ask?  Because it makes the questions of confirmations a
non-issue.  With these new commands, you are very unlikely to hit a
monster you didn't mean to hit, or enter water you didn't mean to enter.
Nothing about the game is given away, either, which I like.  Most novices
are likely to #swim just because the command is there.  Experts know how
to swim if they choose to, but needn't play extra slow just to avoid
typing errors on treacherous levels.

This way, players are held accountable for each and every move, nothing is
given away about the game, and the chance of sudden accidental death is
minimized.


